Article 21

Protection of women and child victims of violence includes: a. prevention;
b. services;
c. empowerment; andd. social rehabilitation

CHAPTER VIII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 32

1. The community may participate in the implementation of protection for women and children.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in the form of:a. forming family partners at the sub-district/village level;
b. forming non-governmental organizations whose field of work is in the field of protection of women and children;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's rights;
d. making prevention efforts if finding indications of violence against women and children in the community;
e. providing first aid to women and children victims of violence; andf. reporting to the competent authority if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, and the business sector.

Article 34

1. The Regional Government encourages the business sector to participate in the protection of women and children.
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:a. socialization to employees within the company environment regarding the rights of women and children;
b. socialization to employees within the company environment regarding the dangers of acts of violence against women and children;
c. providing assistance in the implementation of protection of women and children as a form of corporate social responsibility.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection of women and children is based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of women and children;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of women and children victims of violence; and
e. legal certainty.

Article 25

1. Regional Government is obligated and responsible to provide special protection for children.
2. Special protection for children as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided to:a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. children victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. children victims of sexual crimes;
k. children victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. children victims of maltreatment and neglect; n. children with deviant social behavior; ando. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
3. Provisions regarding the procedures for special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 17

In addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 16, child victims of violence also receive special rights in the form of:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; ande. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 13

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 10 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner;
d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and or certain purposes; and/ore. persuasion, deception and a series of lies that result in sexual violence.

Article 10

Violence against women and children may take the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.

Article 16

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. excellent service;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage; e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; andg. guarantees of rights related to status as a family member as well as a community member and the right to restitution.

