CHAPTER VIII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 32

1. The community may participate in the implementation of protection for women and children.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in the form of:a. forming family partners at the sub-district/village level;
b. forming non-governmental organizations whose field of work is in the field of protection of women and children;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's rights;
d. making prevention efforts if finding indications of violence against women and children in the community;
e. providing first aid to women and children victims of violence; andf. reporting to the competent authority if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, and the business sector.

Article 27

1. For the purpose of recovery, women and children victims, witnesses and perpetrators of violence may obtain services from:a. Social institutions (groups or institutions that have scope in women and children) and or;
b. Social workers (Individuals who have competence to carry out recovery or rehabilitation) namely:
1. Nurses
2. Health workers
3. Counseling workers (2) What is meant by providing recovery services carried out by social institutions as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is as follows:a. volunteer assistants make efforts to:
1. build equal relationships with victims so they are willing to open up in expressing their problems;
2. empathize and not blame victims regarding or related to their problems;
3. convince victims that no one may commit acts of violence;
4. ask what they want to do and what assistance is needed;
5. provide information and connect with institutions or individuals who can help overcome their problems; and/or
6. help provide information about legal consultation servicesb. spiritual counselors make efforts to:
1. explore information and listen to complaints from victims;
2. strengthen the faith and piety of victims and encourage them to carry out worship according to their respective religions and beliefs.
3. suggest solutions to domestic violence problems according to the victim's respective religion and beliefs.
4. provide understanding regarding equality between men and women.
3. What is meant by providing recovery services carried out by social workers as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b has the following provisions:a. explore victims' problems to help solve their problems;
b. restore victims from traumatic conditions through psychosocial therapy;
c. make referrals to hospitals or safe houses or service centers or other alternative places according to the needs of victims;
d. accompany victims in recovery efforts through assistance and counseling; and/ore. carry out resocialization so that victims can return to carrying out their social functions in society.

Article 21

Protection of women and child victims of violence includes: a. prevention;
b. services;
c. empowerment; andd. social rehabilitation

CHAPTER VI

ESTABLISHMENT OF UPTD PPA

Article 30

1. In the context of implementing protection for women and children, the Regional Government establishes UPTD PPA (Protection of Women and Children) as a service center for the protection of women and children.
2. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of elements of regional apparatus that handle affairs of:a. education;
b. youth;
c. social;
d. cooperatives;
e. industry;
f. manpower;
g. trade;
h. population and civil registration;
i. women's empowerment and child protection; j. public peace and order;
k. regional general hospital;
l. health;
3. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.
4. Provisions regarding the organizational structure, duties and functions of UPTD PPA shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER XII

PROHIBITIONS

Article 41

Every person is prohibited from:
a. treating women and children in a discriminatory manner that results in women and children suffering losses, both material and moral, thereby hindering their social function;
b. treating women and children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
c. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
d. placing, allowing, committing, ordering the commission of or participating in committing violence against women and children;
e. committing violence or threat of violence to force women and children to have sexual intercourse with him or with another person;
f. committing violence or threat of violence, coercion, deception, a series of lies, or persuading women and children to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
g. placing, allowing, committing, ordering the commission of or participating in committing kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking of women and children;
h. obstructing women and children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing their religious teachings and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;
i. placing, allowing, committing, ordering the commission of or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of women and children;
j. intentionally placing, allowing, involving women and children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances; and/ork. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of women and children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

